        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "_http_get() in src/picklescan/scanner.py \u2014 reachable from the public scan_url()/scan_huggingface_model() entry points with an attacker-supplied URL \u2014 follows HTTP 302 redirects by recursively calling itself with the raw `Location` header value, with no cap on the number of redirects followed, no allowlist/validation of the redirect destination host/scheme, and no limit on the size of the response body read via `response.read()`. A malicious or compromised server (e.g. a spoofed model-hosting endpoint passed to `scan_url`) can therefore redirect-chain the scanner into internal/private network endpoints (SSRF) or force unbounded memory consumption / recursion via an infinite redirect loop or oversized response \u2014 there is no standard 'bounded redirect following + destination validation + response-size cap' control present anywhere in this function or its callers.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified in real source: _http_get (scanner.py:250-269) recursively follows HTTP 302 redirects by calling itself with the raw, unvalidated Location header (line 262) \u2014 no redirect-depth counter, no host/scheme allowlist \u2014 and returns response.read() (line 267) with no size cap. scan_url (line 625-628) passes its `url` parameter straight into _http_get with zero validation, and CLI (cli.py:66) feeds args.url (direct user input) into scan_url. scan_huggingface_model (574-592) also funnels externally-influenced URLs through the same unbounded _http_get. Graph query confirms all three methods are reachable_from_entry=true/hop_distance=0 and scan_url/scan_huggingface_model are registered EntryPoints with exposure=reachable, kind=handler, matching the analyst's claim exactly."
          },
