        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "scan_huggingface_model(repo_id, ...) interpolates the entry-point parameter `repo_id` unsanitized into HTTPS URLs (f\"https://huggingface.co/api/models/{repo_id}\" and f\"https://huggingface.co/{repo_id}/resolve/main/{file_name}\") that are then fetched via _http_get. Because repo_id is not validated or URL-encoded, a crafted repo_id (containing '/', '..', '#', '?' etc.) can alter the request path/query sent to huggingface.co, and the request still inherits the unbounded redirect-following behavior of _http_get noted above.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified in actual source (not just graph): scan_huggingface_model(repo_id) at src/picklescan/scanner.py:574-592 takes `repo_id` directly from the CLI entry point (cli.py:67-68, `args.huggingface_model` passed with zero validation/encoding) and interpolates it unsanitized into two f-string URLs at line 578 (`f\"https://huggingface.co/api/models/{repo_id}\"`) and lines 588-589 (`url = f\"https://huggingface.co/{repo_id}/resolve/main/{file_name}\"`), both of which go straight into `_http_get` (scanner.py:250-269). `_http_get` parses the URL with `urllib.parse.urlparse`, opens an `HTTPSConnection` to `parsed_url.netloc`, and on any `302` response recurses on `response.headers[\"Location\"]` with no scheme/host allow-list, i.e. unbounded, unchecked redirect-following \u2014 confirming that half of the claim exactly as stated. Graph confirms scan_huggingface_model is an EntryPoint (exposure=reachable, kind=handler) and every relevant CpgCall at lines 578/588/589 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, so exploitability-via-reachability is well supported, not merely assumed.\nHowever, I must correct the severity/impact the lead implies: in BOTH templates the literal text \"huggingface.co/\" (with its terminating slash) precedes the `{repo_id}` substitution point, so per RFC3986/urlparse semantics the authority (netloc) is always fixed to \"huggingface.co\" regardless of what repo_id contains \u2014 a crafted repo_id cannot redirect the outbound TCP connection to an attacker-controlled or internal host. The worst a crafted repo_id (`/`, `..`, `#`, `?`) can do is alter the *path/query sent to huggingface.co itself* (as the lead correctly scopes it), which is a real but low-impact input-validation gap, not full classic SSRF to arbitrary infrastructure. A genuinely dangerous chain would additionally require huggingface.co to serve an open redirect off some path reachable via this injection, which is unverifiable from this repo and purely speculative. Combined with the fact that repo_id is supplied by the same local user invoking the CLI (not a remote/network-exposed server endpoint), real-world exploitability is limited. The code defect is real and exactly where claimed; severity should be downgraded from what \"SSRF/CWE-918\" usually implies."
          },
