        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-22",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "scan_7z_bytes() in src/picklescan/scanner.py extracts archive member names taken directly from an untrusted 7z file (`archive.getnames()`) to a temp directory via `archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets)` and then reconstructs the output path with a plain `os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name)` \u2014 picklescan's own code performs no path-traversal sanitization (no realpath/commonpath containment check) on `file_name` before using it, and no cap on total extracted size/file count (zip-bomb style) is present anywhere in the function, relying entirely on the third-party py7zr library to enforce path containment and resource limits.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the real source directly: scan_7z_bytes (src/picklescan/scanner.py:428-449) takes archive.getnames() (line 438, fully attacker-controlled member names from the 7z being scanned), filters to a `targets` list by suffix only (line 439), calls archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets) (line 442), then rebuilds the path with plain os.path.join(tmpdir, file_name) (line 444) guarded only by os.path.isfile (line 446) before feeding it to scan_file_path (line 447). There is no realpath/commonpath/normpath containment check anywhere in this function or file around it \u2014 confirmed both by reading the code and by a graph query for commonpath/realpath/resolve/abspath/normpath calls in scanner.py around lines 428-449, which returned zero hits in that range (the only abspath hit is the unrelated cli.py:58 one the analyst also found). Reachability is strong, not weak: the CpgMethod for scan_7z_bytes has reachable_from_entry=true and is itself registered as a graph EntryPoint/handler, and it is directly reached from scan_pytorch (scanner.py:525) and scan_bytes (scanner.py:569), which in turn are reached from scan_file_path (reads an arbitrary local file, line 617-622) and scan_url (downloads and scans an arbitrary remote URL, line 625-628) \u2014 i.e. exactly the untrusted-model-file code path that is picklescan's whole purpose. Dependency node confirms py7zr==0.22.0 is pinned. The one real caveat (shared with the analyst, and one I could not resolve myself) is that py7zr's own extract() may already enforce path containment internally; the package isn't vendored/installed in this sandbox and I have no network access to inspect py7zr 0.22.0's internals, and the exploit-reference catalog in this environment returned \"not indexed\" so I could not check it for a matching published module either. That means I can confirm with my own evidence that picklescan's own code has no independent sanitization/size-cap defense (the literal claim made), but I cannot independently confirm or refute whether this is fully weaponizable into an actual write-outside-tmpdir today, since that depends on unverified third-party library behavior."
          },
